// Protocol Buffers for sysl, in pure sysl: the wire-format runtime generated code calls
// (`sh.sysl.protobuf`), a `.proto` reader that resolves a schema into a model
// (`sh.sysl.protobuf.schema`), and a generator that writes that model out as sysl
// (`sh.sysl.protobuf.gen`). The command `protoc-sysl` in `tools/` is a thin layer over the last two.
//
// This is the block a consumer writes to reach it:
//
//     dependencies {
//       protobuf { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/protobuf", version = "<the first tag>" }
//     }
//
// **The runtime needs no operating system and no heap to encode**: a message is written into a slice
// the caller owns, and `tools/portable` builds that for a freestanding target. Reading a `string`,
// `bytes` or repeated field allocates, since what was read has to be put somewhere; the schema reader
// and the generator are hosted code.

package {
  name    = "protobuf"
  version = "0.0.1"
}

dependencies {
  parsing { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/parsing", version = "0.13.0" }
}


// `tools/protoc-sysl` is the command; `tools/portable` is the freestanding probe.
workspace = ["tools/*"]
